{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request a103493a, identifiers (c+d): the ACT producer of a\nreceiver pin stays. Its kind is the kind that the definition declares\nfor the scope field, held as an expectation and checked at the first\ndelivery.\n\nWhy. `nativeLicense` already admits this producer from the declared\nkind of the field, and `capacityOf` funds the record from the same\ndescription. Today `licensed` reads the kind from the value, and the\nvalue's kind must equal the declared kind. So the price already rests\non the declaration. Making the ACT unavailable narrows an admitted\nprogram for no gain.\n\nThis amends item 2 of ruling 8894ef60, which said \"from nowhere else\".\n\n1. A delivery or a created genesis that runs `hold.license` writes\n   `taskKind` from the `sourceKind` of its entry, as 8894ef60 says.\n2. An act that runs `hold.license` with `from: {field}` writes\n   `taskKind` from the kind that the definition declares for that\n   scope field. The scope reads nothing. The record says which of the\n   two it holds, in one member, so a later reader does not take a\n   declaration for a checked kind.\n3. The price reads `taskKind` in both cases.\n4. When a delivery from the record's `task` reaches the pin, the\n   `sourceKind` of that delivery must equal `taskKind`. A difference\n   means that the pin does not cover the delivery, and the delivery\n   gets the answer that it gets today with no pin. The reservation\n   stays as it is. If no site compares a delivery with its pin today,\n   stop and name where the pin is consumed.\n5. A declared kind is never a CheckedSequence, a birth proof or\n   `from.pin` evidence, and it is never copied into a `sourceKind` or a\n   `FactUse.kind`.\n\nThe first owner of receiver-pins.test.ts keeps its ACT and its two\ndeciding amounts. Add one case for item 4: a pin licensed by an act\nfor one kind, and a delivery whose `sourceKind` is another.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}